# ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
# ║  caddyfile.placeholder                                                      ║
# ║                                                                              ║
# ║  The Caddyfile body that caddy-install.sh creates as a Swarm config         ║
# ║  object on each deploy. SHA-rotation per CONSTRAINTS H2: editing this       ║
# ║  file produces a new content hash → new config name → next deploy rolls    ║
# ║  the caddy-edge-ingress service onto the new config.                       ║
# ║                                                                              ║
# ║  Phase 0 scope (per C10 corrected):                                         ║
# ║  - Caddy on rishi-4/5 receives proxied traffic from rishi-1/2/3 edge       ║
# ║    Caddy (currently DEFERRED per A2 — no traffic source yet).              ║
# ║  - This placeholder configuration validates the deploy shape works +       ║
# ║    has the listener + cert scaffolding ready when rishi-1/2/3 lands.       ║
# ║  - Real per-service routes (public-api, orchestrator, hello-world, …)      ║
# ║    arrive when Sessions 3+4 spawn services, via a future                   ║
# ║    `generate-caddy-swarm-config.sh` that materializes routes from a        ║
# ║    template + service registry. Queued as session-1/caddy-swarm-config-    ║
# ║    generator follow-up.                                                    ║
# ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

{
    # Caddy global options. The admin endpoint binds 127.0.0.1:2019 by
    # default — explicit here to match the stack-file healthcheck probe.
    #
    # We do NOT set `auto_https off`. Day-5 Step 4 deploy bug #1 surfaced
    # the gotcha: `auto_https off` sets `automatic_https.disable=true` in
    # Caddy's effective config, which then leaves `tls_connection_policies`
    # empty for all sites — `tls internal` provisioning never runs, so any
    # TLS handshake to :443 fails with a server-side `internal_error`
    # alert (curl exit 35 / openssl SSL alert 80). The intuitive reading
    # of `auto_https off` is "no ACME against public CAs", but it actually
    # disables ALL automatic cert handling including the local-CA path
    # `tls internal` depends on. Since we explicitly use `tls internal`
    # below, we want automatic HTTPS enabled so Caddy provisions the
    # local CA + per-SNI certs on demand. ACME against Let's Encrypt
    # never triggers because the `:443` site below uses `tls internal`
    # explicitly — Caddy honors per-site issuer directives over the
    # global default.
    admin 127.0.0.1:2019
}

# Default site listening on the only published host port (443 via Swarm
# ingress mode). Returns a marker response so curl smoke-tests confirm
# the deploy works without depending on rishi-1/2/3 traffic source.
# `tls internal` makes Caddy generate + serve a self-signed cert via
# its local CA at /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/, which Cloudflare-
# in-Full-mode (the eventual edge configuration) accepts.
#
# IMPORTANT: the site block must pin a HOSTNAME, not a port-only `:443`.
# Day-5 Step 4 deploy bug #3 (resolved under coordinator-override of the
# 1-3-bug budget, same precedent as PR #73): `tls internal` provisions
# a leaf cert from the local CA at TLS-handshake time bound to a SUBJECT
# (hostname). A port-only `:443 { tls internal ... }` site gives Caddy
# no SNI to anchor the cert to — the connection policy is registered but
# the automation policy never issues a leaf, so every handshake aborts
# with `internal_error` (SSL alert 80). `placeholder.rishi.local` is a
# non-routable placeholder until Sessions 3+4 spawn real services (each
# will declare its own site block via the future caddy-swarm-config-
# generator follow-up). Smoke tests use
# `curl --resolve placeholder.rishi.local:443:<ip>` to pin the SNI.
placeholder.rishi.local {
    tls internal
    respond "v2 cluster edge — Phase 0 placeholder; no service routes wired yet" 200
}
